  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1055 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nHot conditions will persist across the Mid-South for the\nforeseeable future. The primary feature driving this pattern is a\npersistent upper-level ridge positioned over the southern United\nStates. Daily triple-digit high temperatures are becoming common\nacross the region. When combined with moderate humidity, heat\nindex values are frequently reaching or exceeding 105\u00c2\u00b0F during\nthe afternoon and early evening hours. Isolated showers and\nthunderstorms will offer little relief, except in localized areas\ndirectly beneath a cell. Expect similar conditions on Saturday\nand Sunday, with dangerous heat indices and high temperatures\nclimbing into the triple digits across much of the area. A Heat\nAdvisory remains in effect through 8 PM Sunday.\n\nAn upper-level trough digging into the northeastern U.S. will\npush a backdoor cold front through the region on Sunday. However,\nnoticeable relief will be delayed until drier air filters in on\nnortheasterly surface winds on Monday. Dewpoints are expected to\ndrop into the 60s, keeping heat index values below advisory\ncriteria. Because dry air warms efficiently, afternoon highs will\nstill reach the mid-to-upper 90s, with low 100s possible across\nportions of the Mississippi Delta on Monday afternoon.\n\nThe upper-level ridge strengthens over the region Tuesday through\nThursday, while humidity levels gradually increase under light\nsoutherly flow. High temperatures will remain well above normal,\nranging from 95\u00c2\u00b0F to 100\u00c2\u00b0F. Heat index values may approach\nadvisory thresholds during this period, and trends will be\nmonitored closely.\n\nBy late week, another upper-level shortwave digging into the\nGreat Lakes and Northeast may push a cold front toward the Mid-\nSouth. This will bring low chances for rain along with slightly\nlower humidity and temperatures, though no significant cooldown\nis anticipated. Medium-range guidance indicates the upper ridge\nwill quickly rebuild next weekend into the following week.\nOverall, hot and predominantly dry weather will continue into mid-\nSeptember and potentially beyond, with drought conditions\nworsening.",
